              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Making eye contact with someone who has a lazy eye or crossed eyes (medically known as **strabismus**) can sometimes feel socially tricky. It is incredibly common to feel unsure of where to look, but with a few simple techniques, you can make the interaction comfortable, respectful, and natural for both of you. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Focus on the eye that is looking at you\nWhile one of their eyes may wander or point in a different direction, the other eye (the dominant eye) is aligned and looking directly at you. \n* **What to do:** Identify the eye that is making direct contact with you and focus your gaze on that one. From their perspective, this feels like entirely normal, direct eye contact.\n\n### 2. Look at the bridge of their nose\nThis is a classic conversational trick that works for any social interaction, but it is especially helpful here.\n* **What to do:** Focus your gaze on the bridge of their nose, right between their eyes, or slightly above their brow line. \n* **Why it works:** To the other person, it will look like you are making direct eye contact. For you, it removes the pressure of trying to choose which eye to look at and keeps your gaze steady.\n\n### 3. Pick one spot and stick to it (don't \"dart\")\nThe most common mistake people make is letting their eyes dart back and forth between the person's left and right eye, trying to figure out where to look. \n* **Why to avoid this:** People with strabismus are highly aware of their condition and can easily spot when someone's eyes are rapidly shifting. It can make them feel self-conscious or signal to them that you are distracted by their eyes rather than listening to what they are saying. \n* **What to do:** Once you choose an eye or the bridge of their nose, keep your gaze anchored there.\n\n### 4. Look at their whole face\nEye contact doesn't mean locking eyes intensely for the entire conversation. Healthy communication involves looking at the speaker\u2019s mouth, their smile, their hand gestures, and their overall facial expressions. \n* **What to do:** Soften your focus to take in their entire face. This keeps the conversation feeling warm, natural, and relaxed.\n\n### 5. Break eye contact naturally when you speak\nIn normal conversations, people naturally look away when they are thinking or speaking, and look back when they are listening. Use this to your advantage. When you are the one talking, feel free to let your gaze drift naturally before returning to their dominant eye or the bridge of their nose when they start speaking again.\n\n---\n\n### What to Avoid (Social Etiquette)\n* **Never look behind you:** If you aren't sure if they are looking at you, do not look over your shoulder to see if they are addressing someone else. Trust that if they are facing you and speaking, they are looking at you.\n* **Never ask, \"Are you looking at me?\":** This can be deeply embarrassing and hurtful to someone with strabismus.\n* **Don't point it out:** Unless they bring it up themselves, there is no need to mention their eye. \n\n**The Golden Rule:** Remember that they are looking at you, even if their eyes don't line up perfectly. Treat them with the exact same warmth, respect, and attention you would give to anyone else.",
